<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Adverbs - Job and effect</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
    <h2>Adverb or preposition?</h2>
    <p>Some words change job. Look at what follows them.</p>
    <p>“We played <strong>outside</strong>.” <em>Outside</em> tells where we played. It is an adverb.</p>
    <p>“We played <strong>outside the house</strong>.” <em>Outside</em> is followed by the noun phrase <em>the house</em>, so it is a preposition.</p>
    <p>“We left <strong>afterwards</strong>.” <em>Afterwards</em> tells when. It is a time adverb.</p>
    <p>“We left <strong>after lunch</strong>.” <em>After</em> is followed by the noun phrase <em>lunch</em>, so it is a preposition.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
    <h2>The adverb changes the picture</h2>
    <p>He <strong>carefully</strong> opened the box. He took care.</p>
    <p>He <strong>eagerly</strong> opened the box. He was excited to see what was inside.</p>
    <p>She <strong>rarely</strong> visits. She does not visit often. She <strong>often</strong> visits. She visits frequently.</p>
    <p><em>Carefully</em> and <em>eagerly</em> both tell how, but they create different impressions. Explain the effect, not only the type.</p>
  </section>
</div>
